-- front.pack -- the front of a cloud: the program that ends TLS, placed on a machine (FRONT-1).
--
-- Caddy 2.11.7 (STZ-OS-RULING-18, vendored by digest: vendor/PIN.md, fetched by
-- experiment/caddy_fetch.sh), one static binary that manages its own certificates. The
-- estate's rule is that no repository vendors a TLS stack and TLS ends at a proxy in
-- front; this is that proxy, as a world like any other server: an identity of its own,
-- the directories it owns, the ports it binds and no other.
--
-- What it asks, and where each need was read (Caddy 2.11.7's docs and its first run
-- outside the floor, experiment/front_outside.sh):
--   the network      443 for TLS, 80 for the redirect automatic HTTPS makes, and
--                    127.0.0.1:8080 for the one plain answer it gives, /health, which
--                    is what `harb ready` asks; nothing else is bound (PORT-1 holds it)
--   the filesystem and the mount `data`   its storage -- the intermediate it signs
--                    with, the leaves it issues -- its own log, and the ROOT it was given
--   a place of its own   /data/front for all of that, /run/front for the word that it
--                    serves. The root certificate and key are NOT generated by the floor
--                    or by Caddy: the scene makes them (openssl) and seeds them on the
--                    disk, owned by this identity, before the first boot -- enrolment by
--                    hand, as a device's key is (FLT-1); the fleet declares the root by
--                    its fingerprint
--   threads          Go's runtime: a worker per processor and its own housekeeping
--   a clock          a certificate is a dated claim; the machine has no trusted clock
--                    and says so elsewhere (STZ-OS-RULING-07)
--   process          the readiness program starts it
-- and what it does not ask: no environment (it has none, so its storage and log are
-- named in its configuration, never in $HOME), no admin endpoint (off), no trust-store
-- installation (skipped: a client trusts what it is given).
--
-- Its console is muted (`--mute`): a server that logs every request to stderr would
-- write the machine's transcript, and what it keeps of its own is /data/front/caddy.log.

DEFINE USER frontdoor AS (
  UID 2020
) RATIONALE "The identity the front runs as: it owns its storage and its log, binds the ports it was given, and nothing else of the machine"

DEFINE SERVICE front AS (
  RUN [
    "/harb", "ready", "/run/front/ready", "127.0.0.1:8080",
    "--every", "2", "--mute", "--",
    "/caddy", "run", "--config", "/app/Caddyfile", "--adapter", "caddyfile"
  ],
  RESTART always,
  READY "/run/front/ready",
  HEALTH 10,
  NEEDS [network, filesystem, process, threads, clock],
  SEES [data],
  USER frontdoor,
  STATE ["/run/front", "/data/front"],
  PORTS [80, 443, 8080],
  MEMORY 128,
  CPU 100,
  TASKS 64
) RATIONALE "The front, as an identity of its own: it serves once its own plain /health answers on the loopback and says so by the path PID 1 waits for, every 2 seconds for as long as it keeps answering; it binds 443, 80 and its loopback 8080 and is refused every other port; past 128 MiB it is stopped alone, past one core it waits, past 64 tasks it is refused more"
